  <div id="sutta">
  
            <h4 id="thag1.88" class="h1">Thag 1:88  Ajjuna</h4>
            <div class="verse">
              <p>Able was I</p>
              <p>to raise myself from water</p>
              <p class="v2">to dry land.<span class="fn"><a href="#thag1.88note01">1</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
              <p>Swept along by the great flood,</p>
              <p class="v2">I penetrated</p>
              <p class="v2">the truths.<span class="fn"><a href="#thag1.88note02">2</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
            </div><!--end verse-->
            <div class="note">
              <p class="notetitle">Notes</p><!--end notetitle-->
              <p id="thag1.88note01">1. A standard image for the practice is that of making one’s way across a river from its dangerous near shore to the safety of the further shore. For other uses of this image, see <a href="../../SN/SN35_197.html">SN 35:197</a>, <a href="../../AN/AN4_5.html">AN 4:5</a>, <a href="../../AN/AN7_15.html">AN 7:15</a>, and <a href="../StNp/StNp5_intro.html">Sn 5</a>.</p>
              <p id="thag1.88note02">2. The four noble truths.</p>
            </div><!--end notes-->
      </div><!--end:sutta-->
    </div><!--end:content-->
    
